  "stable_key": "g4-lesson-01",
  "position": 1,
  "title": "Use of Pythagoras’ theorem",
  "summary": "Use the relationship between the three sides of a right triangle to calculate missing lengths and solve contextual problems.",
  "outcomes": [
    "4.1"
  ],
  "prerequisite_lessons": [],
  "estimated_minutes": 60,
  "objectives": [
    "Identify the hypotenuse and the two perpendicular sides.",
    "Calculate a missing hypotenuse or shorter side.",
    "Model diagonals, heights and straight-line distances with right triangles."
  ],
  "sections": [
    {
      "stable_key": "g4-41-relationship",
      "position": 1,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "The relationship in a right triangle",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Pythagoras’ theorem applies only to a right-angled triangle. Label the side opposite the right angle c; it is the hypotenuse and is always the longest side. If the perpendicular sides are a and b, then a² + b² = c²."
        },
        {
          "type": "display_math",
          "latex": "a^2+b^2=c^2"
        },
        {
          "type": "asset_ref",
          "asset_key": "g4-lesson-01-pythagoras"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g4-41-hypotenuse",
      "position": 2,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Finding the hypotenuse",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "When both shorter sides are known, square each length, add, and take the positive square root. Lengths cannot be negative. Keep the exact square-root form during working and round only if the question requests it."
        },
        {
          "type": "display_math",
          "latex": "c=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g4-41-example-hyp",
      "position": 3,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: diagonal",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A rectangular courtyard is 9 m by 12 m. Find its diagonal."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The diagonal is the hypotenuse of a right triangle."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "d^2=9^2+12^2"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "d=\\sqrt{225}=15\\text{ m}"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "The diagonal exceeds both side lengths and 9² + 12² = 15²."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g4-41-shorter",
      "position": 4,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Finding a shorter side",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "If the hypotenuse and one shorter side are known, subtract the known shorter-side square from the hypotenuse square. Adding would produce a result longer than the hypotenuse and cannot be correct."
        },
        {
          "type": "display_math",
          "latex": "a=\\sqrt{c^2-b^2}"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g4-41-example-leg",
      "position": 5,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: ladder height",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A 13 m ladder reaches a wall with its foot 5 m from the wall. Find the vertical height reached."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "h^2+5^2=13^2"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "h=\\sqrt{169-25}=12\\text{ m}"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "The answer is shorter than the 13 m ladder, and 5² + 12² = 13²."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g4-41-model",
      "position": 6,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Build the right triangle from context",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Draw a simple sketch before substituting. Vertical and horizontal directions are perpendicular, and a rectangle’s diagonal creates two right triangles. Separate any extra cable, border or path length from the Pythagoras calculation."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Use consistent units before squaring. Squared centimetres and squared metres cannot be added, even though the final result is a length."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g4-41-recall",
      "position": 7,
      "type": "active_recall",
      "title": "Check your understanding",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A right triangle has shorter sides 8 and 15. Enter the hypotenuse."
        }
      ],
      "response": {
        "type": "numeric",
        "comparison_mode": "exact_numeric",
        "canonical_answer": "17",
        "canonical_latex": "17",
        "accepted_answers": [],
        "absolute_tolerance": null,
        "rounding_precision": null,
        "unit": null
      },
      "feedback": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "8² + 15² = 64 + 225 = 289 = 17²."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g4-lesson-01-summary",
      "position": 8,
      "type": "summary",
      "title": "Lesson checklist",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Mark the right angle, label the hypotenuse opposite it, and decide whether to add squares or subtract one square."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Check units, take the positive square root, and confirm that the hypotenuse is the longest side."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
